\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Ctrl Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Policy Questioned At Bank's Event   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael Casey   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,134 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2007\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's two-day conference, which ended Tuesday, featured heavyweight monetary chiefs and private economists, some of whom took issue with their host's unorthodox \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.   \par \par \uc2 The speaker list included four current members of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as the ECB's former Chief Economist, Otmar Issing, the presidents of the Russian, Indian and Chilean central banks, and the chief economist of the  \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of New York\uc2 , Joseph Tracy. The line-up also included big-name university economists such as Columbia's Joseph Stiglitz, Harvard's Richard Cooper, Chicago's Raghuram Rajan and Stanford's Ronald McKinnon.   \par \par \uc2 How did Argentina's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , hardly a world behemoth, afford the gala event?   \par \par \uc2 The answer can be found in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet, in an item that was in fact the focus of a polemical debate in the conference: its foreign reserves.   \par \par \uc2 Since Martin Redrado succeeded Alfonso Prat-Gay as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's president in September 2004, reserves have grown from around $18 billion to more than $40 billion, even after accounting for a $9.6 billion withdrawal to pay off International Monetary Fund in early 2005. Those holdings explain a big chunk of the Bank's 6.5% return on investment, a figure Redrado cited in opening remarks Monday.   \par \par \uc2 But as some speakers pointed out, Argentines could be paying a high price for this expansion in reserves, which stem from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy, daily dollar-buying in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. These \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have kept the peso more or less unchanged at around ARS3.00 to the dollar for three years, in line with the oft-stated objectives of the federal government.   \par \par \uc2 As he always does, Redrado described the policy as one of "reserve accumulation," thus sidestepping the critique that his ostensibly independent institution is following the policy guidelines of the federal government.   \par \par \uc2 Building reserves, he said, provided a "prudent" buffer against external shocks and volatility, so long as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary objectives are met. These targets, defined this year as an expansion in the M2 monetary base of between 11.7% and 18.7%, have been repeatedly attained while the real growth in money supply has been brought to below that of GDP for the first time since the country's financial crisis of 2002, Redrado said.   \par \par \uc2 But other speakers turned the logic on its head, treating reserve growth as a byproduct of a de facto fix in the exchange rate, not a policy in itself. And this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fix runs the risk of generating sustained inflation and financial instability over the medium term, some warned.   \par \par \uc2 "Now, I don't want to offend anyone, but Argentina is targeting its exchange rate," said Stanford University's McKinnon. "They say they have a money supply rule or something like that, but in fact the exchange rate is stable...so, it is as if the money goal was the instrument but the target is the exchange rate."   \par \par \uc2 By preventing the peso from naturally appreciating, McKinnon said, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has postponed the real, inflation-adjusted exchange rate's return to the purchasing power parity it had before the crisis. The continued differential between the undervalued nominal exchange rate and that equilibrium rate has undermined the effectiveness of the banking system, where credit growth is weak, and has left the economy with pent-up inflation, McKinnon added.   \par \par \uc2 Admitting that he doesn't have "a clean solution" for restoring equilibrium at this late stage, the economist said the delay in nominal exchange rate appreciation had put the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bind.   \par \par \uc2 It can't simply tell the world it will now allow the peso to gradually appreciate, he said, because "the announcement effect" will attract a massive inflow of "hot money" capital seeking to reap the guaranteed exchange rate gains, he said. As with the problems that China faces with its gradualist yuan appreciation strategy, these inflows would complicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to manage the growth in Argentina's monetary base, McKinnon said.   \par \par \uc2 As it is, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy of buying up almost all the dollars coming into \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already creating monetary policy problems.   \par \par \uc2 Its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are paid for with large-scale injections of pesos into the system, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must then turn around and "sterilize" through absorption \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The result has been an ever-expanding portfolio of short- and medium-term money market liabilities. This creates a constant refinancing treadmill, where the task of maintaining investor demand without jacking up interest rates is challenged by the inflationary environment.   \par \par \uc2 Esteban Medrano, an economist at local consultancy  \uc2 Macrovision\uc2 , said the offsetting issuance of \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  securities means the reserve accumulation policy is better described as "reserve borrowing" policy. It's effectively financed by loans from the financial sector.   \par \par \uc2 The rates paid on the longer-term liabilities are considerably higher than the interest earned on reserves, since Argentine inflation is high by world standards, he noted. The consumer price index is up 8.8% year-on-year in official terms but real inflation is thought to be well above 10%, given widespread mistrust of the government data.   \par \par \uc2 Medrano said the negative spread between the rates paid on the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's liabilities over its assets will sooner or later put an end to the profitability helping to pay for events such as this week's conference.   \par \par \uc2 The only way to stop this vicious cycle, many economists say, is to rapidly revalue the real exchange rate. That can be done via the nominal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would seriously hurt both domestic exporters - whose tax payments form a crucial pillar in the government's fiscal surplus - as well as employers in the import-competing industries that have blossomed in the post-crisis era. Alternatively, the country can inflate its way to real exchange rate equilibrium, creating financial stability that deters both savings and investment.   \par \par \uc2 But judging from his closing remarks Tuesday, Redrado has no intention of changing course.   \par \par \uc2 In an apparent critique of McKinnon, who compared Argentina's situation to the better-managed policies of South Korea after its 1998 crisis, Redrado said "simplistic comparisons between...diverse countries...can result in totally erroneous recommendations."   \par \par \uc2 Citing the Chilean experience as a model, Redrado lauded that country's use of an "exchange rate anchor" in its initial transition to an inflation-targeting regime. He also argued that Argentina's experience showed that "there is nothing more procyclical than an appreciated local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," which stokes both private and public consumption.   \par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com; 54-11-4313 1918 [ 06-06-07 2000GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020070606e366000y7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
