\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Seen Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,028 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 2007\par 16:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Drew Benson \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso is expected to continue its slide against the dollar in 2007, pushed weaker by continued heavy dollar buying by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest survey of economists, out last week, sees the peso closing 2007 at ARS3.18 against the dollar, a 3.5% decline from last year's close at ARS3.07.  \par \par \uc2 That nominal depreciation will likely be more than offset by inflation, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey sees rising by 10% during the full-year, leading to a real appreciation of the peso this year.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars on a regular basis, making the peso one of the least volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region. While there are occasional blips stemming from movements in the Brazilian real and other neighboring \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the outlook for the peso depends mostly on an analysis of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's capacity to balance the effect of its daily \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the effects they have on monetary conditions.  \par \par \uc2 Officially, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it buys dollars regularly to build reserves, but it is widely seen as a de facto industrial policy, one that is implemented to offset real appreciation from inflation and maintain a relatively competitive peso in favor of Argentine exports.  \par \par \uc2 Inflation came in at 9.8% last year after jumping to 12.3% in 2005, representing a loss of competitiveness over that time. As of Thursday's close, the peso was at ARS3.08 against the dollar, while reserves were at $32.57 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Since daily dollar buying pumps pesos into the money supply, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must use various "sterilization" measures to reabsorb the excess \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the system so as to avoid runaway inflation.  \par \par \uc2 The extent to which it succeeds in this effort could, in the long run, become a factor in the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If monetary expansion were to get out of control, a loss of confidence in the peso itself could transpire, which could leave the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  facing the opposite problem: a vicious spiral in which rising prices and rapidly falling exchange rates feed off each other.  \par \par \uc2 For now, though, the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks to have the key variables relatively well controlled.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not target inflation, but instead sets money supply targets based on M2, which is comprised of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in circulation plus checking and savings deposits.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2007 monetary plan, presented to legislators last month by bank president Martin Redrado, sets an 11.7%-to-18.7% target expansion range for M2 money supply. That's marginally narrower than last year's 11.7%-to-21.2% goal, and puts the upper-end band slightly below the actual 2006 expansion rate of 18.9%. Even so, it still leaves a wide target for the bank to hit.  \par \par \uc2 Most observers say money expansion should, much like last year, come in just under the upper end of the target in 2007, closely following nominal GDP growth. It suggests inflation will remain high compared with other countries but comfortably close around the relatively benign 10% figure.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not without challenges. One problem lies in the dwindling choices it has for sterilizing the pesos it injects into the system with each dollar purchase.  \par \par \uc2 With commercial banks improving their balance sheets, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon be unable to count on one unique source of monetary absorption that has greatly aided its monetary control efforts: a regular transfer from the banks in repayment of discount loans issued during the 2001 and 2002 financial crisis.  \par \par \uc2 That leaves it with fewer sterilization tools. One option still available if money growth gets too fast, would be to announce another hike in reserve requirements. But that would push effective money market interest rates higher - a politically unpopular move - at a time when an expected slowdown in emerging market economies might not warrant such a tightening.  \par \par \uc2 More likely is the use of bigger weekly auctions of Nobac notes and Lebac certificates.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  already did so this week with the introduction of a three-year Nobac that pays 2.5% above the 30-day moving Badlar average of Argentine certificates of deposit. A positive market response to that issue suggested that it currently has little to fear if it wants to adopt a more aggressive sterilization program.  \par \par \uc2 The three-year Nobacs, of which just $25 million were initially offered, drew a record 2.8 billion pesos ($908 million) in bids, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to issue ARS324 million in the new notes.  \par \par \uc2 But such strong results, partially a function of the seasonal boost in liquidity that comes in January with the conclusion of the holiday and bonus period, won't always be possible.  \par \par \uc2 Demand for \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  notes could also dry up, said Sebastian Di Nucci, a portfolio manager in Buenos Aires with FDI consultants, if other investment assets become more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 "Before stocks and bonds began sliding in May of last year, there were weeks during which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  struggled to place notes," Di Nucci said.  \par \par \uc2 If that scenario reemerges, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may also have to hike rates to get its Lebacs and Nobacs out to market. Aside from contradicting an official government policy against rate hikes, this could also draw in foreign capital and compromise the exchange rate goals, requiring more dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and putting the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on a difficult, accelerating treadmill.  \par \par \uc2 This threat is one reason why economists see the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintaining a relatively lax approach to the M2 goals.  \par \par \uc2 "The only way to balance all of the policy goals is to come in closer to the upper band of the target," said Pablo Morra, an economist for Latin America with Goldman Sachs in New York. "That will allow them to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sterilize at the lowest cost in terms of interest rates."  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Drew Benson covers Argentine economic policy, politics, bonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and energy issues.) [ 12-01-0