\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina stocks close at record high, trade booms  \b0\par\par\uc2 271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 2006\par 21:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates to close)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Dec 5 (Reuters) - Argentine stocks closed up 0.99 percent on Tuesday to reach their second record high in less than a week on buying by institutional investors, buoyed by gains in the U.S. market, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark MerVal index <.MERV> of 13 leading stocks closed at 1,977.16 points, eclipsing the previous record close of 1,967.02 on Nov. 30. Shares also notched a new intraday high of 1,981.07.  \par \par \uc2 Trade volume was a hefty 137.8 million pesos ($45 million), compared with an average of about 65 million pesos per day last week. This was the largest volume traded since early March.  \par \par \uc2 "We were in line with the upward trend in external markets. There is a still a boom in liquidity, perhaps because of the premise that U.S. (interest) rates could be cut shortly," said Marcelo Paccione, analyst at ConsulCapital consultancy.  \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> closed higher on Tuesday, as did Brazil's Bovespa <.BVSP> and Mexico's benchmark IPC index <.MXX>.  \par \par \uc2 The MerVal's gains were led by bank shares, including Grupo Financiero Galicia , which jumped 3.3 percent to end at 2.53 pesos, and BBVA Banco Frances , which rose 2.2 percent to 9.4 pesos.  \par \par \uc2 The peso <ARSB=> firmed 0.24 percent to 3.06/3.0625 per dollar, maintaining its strongest levels in six months despite regular \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 ARGENTINA\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|RNP|DNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020061205e2c5001tf\par }\p