\par\par \b \uc2 IMF raises Latam growth on domestic demand, exports  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gilbert Le Gras  \par \uc2 660 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 September 2006\par 04:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 14 (Reuters) - Latin America's economy should expand 4.75 percent this year, up from a prior forecast of 4.3 percent, on firm domestic demand and high prices for key export commodities, the International Monetary Fund said on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 But the region with the world's second-largest oil and gas reserves has for the most part failed to capitalise on a doubling in crude prices in the past two years to either boost output or invest, it said in its twice-yearly review of the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 Brazil, however, is reaping the benefits of long-term investments by Petrobras which have turned a country that had long been an energy importer into a self-sufficient one in 2006.  \par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product in the region's biggest economy should expand 3.6 percent this year and 4.0 percent in 2007, both increases from the IMF's April forecast, while inflation should slow from 4.5 percent in 2006 to 4.1 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 Two weeks ago Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark lending rate by 0.5 percentage point to a 20-year low of 14.25 percent, the 10th consecutive rate cut since September 2005. It is targetting inflation at 4.5 percent in 2007 and 2008.  \par \par \uc2 "In rapidly growing Argentina, the monetary policy stance has been gradually tightened in response to double-digit inflation but remains accommodative," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 Argentine inflation should be 12.3 percent this year before slowing to 11.4 percent in 2007 while growth is forecast to be 8.0 percent in 2006 and 6.0 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 "The use of regulatory counter-measures will need to be supported by a further tightening of macroeconomic policies to contain inflation," it added.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF urged "adequate \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate flexibility" in managing foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows and warned that sustained sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have heavy "quasi-fiscal" costs.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to build reserves and keep the peso weak for exports at around 3 per dollar while Venezuela fixes the bolivar at 2,150 per dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Officials in Caracas last month said they were analysing a possible restructuring to cut up to three zeros off the bolivar.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF openly chided Venezuela and Mexico for low rates of investment in their energy sectors and for only beginning to address governance issues. Both have state-owned energy firms.  \par \par \uc2 Venezuela, the world's fifth-largest oil exporter, should grow 7.5 percent this year and 3.7 percent in 2007. Inflation should speed up from 12.1 percent in 2006 to 15.4 percent in 2007.  \par \par \uc2 Mexico, which only recently boosted investment to make up for dwindling output at its largest oil field, should see GDP growth of 4.0 percent in 2006 and 3.5 percent next year, it said.  \par \par \uc2 Mexican inflation should be 3.5 percent this year and 3.3 percent in 2007 while the current account deficit should narrow to 0.1 percent of GDP in 2006 and widen to 0.2 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 Most South American countries should have current accounts in surplus except for Colombia where the deficit should be 1.2 percent of GDP this year before growing to 1.7 percent in 2007 and Uruguay where it should narrow from 4.3 percent in 2006 to 3.2 percent next year.  \par \par \uc2 Government spending has increased over the past two years across the region and the IMF said it was concerned that not all of it was well targeted.  \par \par \uc2 Some countries have renewed reform efforts after the financial crises in the late 1990s and beginning of this decade and the IMF urged greater openness to trade across the region's economies.  \par \par \uc2 IMF-LATAM (URGENT)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|O|OIL|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|Z|J|U|NAT|SI|RNP|DNP|PTD  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060914e29e0003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
