\par\par \b \uc2 Argentina Bonds Little Changed;Peso Flat Amid Low Activity  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2006\par 21:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Locally traded Argentine bonds were little changed Monday, while the peso closed flat on the day at ARS3.0950, as local markets saw little activity due to the U.S. Labor Day holiday.  \par \par \uc2 "It is very calm," said Alejandro Barros, a financial analyst with local Banco Piano. "Some notes are down slightly, but there isn't much happening."  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Discount bond in pesos was recently quoted at ARS126.65, off slightly from Friday's close at ARS126.75, while the Par in pesos was unchanged at ARS49.55.  \par \par \uc2 The Discount saw just over ARS4 million in volume traded Monday, well below the ARS20 million that normally exchanges hands.  \par \par \uc2 Peso bonds linked to inflation, such as the Discount and Par notes, could take a hit this week following Tuesday's release of August inflation data. Inflation is expected to have shown more signs of moderation in August, and a Dow Jones Newswires survey produced a median forecast of a 0.5% increase in the consumer price index. That forecast comes in below the 0.7% estimated in a month-old survey conducted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  apparently abstained from its daily dollar buying.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  habitually buys dollars to build reserves and maintain a weaker peso.  \par \par \uc2 Economy Minister Felisa Miceli again defended that policy Monday, saying that regular dollar buying will continue. Over the weekend, Miceli estimated that without steady \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the peso would strengthen against the dollar to ARS2.30.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 04-09-06 1949GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 The Argentine peso hit the ARS3.10 mark against the dollar Tuesday, its weakest point in three years, on heavy last-minute dollar buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, locally traded bond prices were higher across the curve, albeit amid mild volumes.  \par \par \uc2 The peso closed at ARS3.10, weakening from ARS3.0950 on Monday, amid normal volumes. Tuesday's close marks the peso's weakest point since mid-March 2003, when the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid as far as ARS3.1250 against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  habitually buys dollars to build reserves and maintain a weak peso that favors Argentine exports. Officials, including Economy Minister Felisa Miceli, have recently reiterated their defense of this plan.  \par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stopped reporting its daily dollar buying in July, local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Hector Blanco of local brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambio estimated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought roughly $80 million during the last 15 minutes of the session to push the peso into weaker territory.  \par \par \uc2 Miceli said Monday that regular dollar buying will continue, estimating that without steady \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  the peso would strengthen to ARS2.30 against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to recovering the $9.5 billion that the government tapped in January to entirely pay down its debt with the International Monetary Fund, daily \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely increase total reserves on the year as President Nestor Kirchner heads toward his 2007 re-election bid, Blanco said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's late dollar buying Tuesday came after the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars earlier in the day.  \par \par \uc2 "It sold cash dollars at ARS3.0950, ARS3.0960 this morning and then at the last minute paid cash at ARS3.0990," Blanco said. "There must have been some financial transaction that isn't clear at the moment."  \par \par \uc2 Total volume in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market reached $237 million.  \par \par \uc2 In the local bond market, "prices are generally all up," said Paulino Seoane, an analyst in Buenos Aires with Lopez Leon Brokers.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Discount in pesos was up to ARS126.85 from ARS126.60, while the Par in pesos was up to ARS49.75 from ARS49.60. At the shorter end of the curve, the Bocon Pre 12, another peso note, was also higher at ARS176.20 from ARS175.50.  \par \par \uc2 Peso notes are linked to inflation. The government announced late in the session Tuesday that the consumer price index rose 0.6% in August, higher than a 0.5% increase predicted last week by economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 Among dollar notes, the Boden 2012 was up to ARS287.80 from ARS287.40.  \par \par \uc2 "There's not much volume, as usual," Seoane added.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Corrected September 5, 2006 16:23 ET (20:23 GMT) [ 05-09-06 1947GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 (The item "\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  Peso Hits ARS3.10 On \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Up -2-" published at 3:47 p.m. EDT, omitted paragraphs 1-5.)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine peso hit the ARS3.10 mark against the dollar Tuesday, its weakest point in three years, on 