\par\par \b \uc2 IMF: Argentina Needs Tighter Budget To Cut Inflation  \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 August 2006\par 20:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON (Dow Jones)--Consumer price inflation in Argentina will probably accelerate if Argentina's government doesn't restrain public spending and tighten monetary policy, the International Monetary Fund said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Though the economy appears likely to continue expanding rapidly as it has since Argentina began recovering from a financial crisis in 2001, inflation "remains high," the IMF said in a press summary of its annual Article IV review of Argentina's economy. Argentina needs to control public spending, raise real interest rates above zero and allow "for a greater upward flexibility in the exchange rate," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 The IMF "regarded current inflation as more a consequence of aggregate demand growth that was not being met by a sufficiently rapid supply response than as a temporary phenomenon arising from a delayed relative price adjustment after the 2002 crisis," the IMF said. "Achieving a soft landing for the economy requires a different macroeconomic policy mix as well as reforms to promote investment and supply, especially in areas where bottlenecks are of macroeconomic significance."  \par \par \uc2 The IMF noted that core inflation is now running just above 12%, and formal private-sector wages are rising.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina's recovery from crisis, which has included sharp declines in unemployment and poverty, a strengthening banking system, rebuilding of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and repayment of IMF obligations, coincided with a massive adjustment in the government's budget. The overall public sector posted a budget surplus of 2.4% of gross domestic product in 2005, compared with a deficit of 5.9% in 2001.  \par \par \uc2 Public spending is now rising, stimulating demand. Non-interest government spending rose by 11% in real terms, eroding the primary surplus as a share of the economy. Provincial spending has been growing 19%, adjusted for inflation.  \par \par \uc2 "Early indicators imply that current trends are set to continue this year," the IMF said. "Public sector capital spending continues to rise rapidly, growing at a real rate of 70% in the 12 months to May."  \par \par \uc2 Argentina's interest rates are "accommodative," the IMF said. The IMF "underscored that all of the available policy instruments should be brought to bear to allow for a soft landing with sustainable growth and low inflation."  \par \par \uc2 The value of \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been stable, amid sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the IMF said. A strengthening peso could also help damp demand and keep prices in check.  \par \par \uc2 Some IMF directors suggested government price controls can help contain inflation expectations, but the benefits are only temporary, the IMF said. Most IMF directors believed the price controls will ultimately worsen inflation because they make supply shortages worse.  \par \par \uc2 "Most directors advised that such administrative measures should be dismantled as soon as possible, since repressing prices in regulated industries, selective price agreements, and export restraints would ultimately exacerbate capacity constraints in key sectors and undermine the business climate," the IMF said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Elizabeth Price, Dow Jones Newswires; 202-862-9249; Elizabeth.Price@dowjones.com [ 09-08-06 1800GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060809e28900133\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
