\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Mixed; Peso Flat On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 410 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 August 2006\par 22:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine bond prices were mixed Friday, as longer-duration notes followed U.S. Treasury prices higher while shorter-term notes slid on lower-than-expected Argentine inflation data for July.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso ended unchanged on the day at ARS3.0700 as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again bought pesos to offset demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Volumes remained generally thin in the local bond market, however. Among gainers, the dollar-denominated Boden 2012 was recently quoted at $70, up from $69.70 at Thursday's close.  \par \par \uc2 The government made principal payments on the Boden 12 Thursday, and many investors were apparently reinvesting in the note, said Adolfo Zuberbuhler, an analyst at local Banco Finansur.  \par \par \uc2 "I think we are going to see more appetite for dollar bonds than for shorter-term (inflation-linked) peso notes," Zuberbuhler said. Investors seeking peso investments will likely move from short-term peso bonds to the floating-rate Nobac notes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  auctions on a weekly basis, he said. Argentina's statistics agency, Indec, announced Thursday that the consumer price index rose 0.6% in July on the month. That came in below expectations, making the inflation-linked bonds less attractive, Zuberbuhler said.  \par \par \uc2 Among shorter-term losers Friday, the Bocon Pre 9 was down at ARS110.05 from ARS110.40, while the Bocon Pre 8 was at ARS191.00, down from ARS192.10.  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark Discount bond in pesos was higher, however, rising to ARS125.40, up from ARS124.00, as emerging market debt benefited from tame U.S. employment data out Friday.  \par \par \uc2 In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought dollars to offset peso demand and rebuild reserves that the government tapped for this week's Boden payout.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the overnight call money rate eased some Friday, to around 8.5% from 9% Thursday. Liquidity tightened this week after a reserves ratio hike went into place Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Beginning Aug. 1, banks must hold 19% of depositors' checking and savings account funds in reserve at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The reserve ratio for these "sight," or current, accounts had been 17% since March, when it rose from 15%.  \par \par \uc2 Before the change tightened liquidity, the overnight call money rate had been around 7.5%. The rate rose as high as 9.5% on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 04-08-06 2031GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1833  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060804e28400131\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
