\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Continue Climb;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Softens Peso  \b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Juli 2006\par 21:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Locally traded Argentine bonds continued to rebound Thursday as U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke made dovish comments for a second straight day.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso ended at ARS3.0825 against the dollar, slightly weaker than Wednesday's close at ARS3.0800.  \par \par \uc2 The local bond market didn't immediately react to the release of the minutes to last month's Fed meeting, which failed to trigger more U.S. Treasurys gains.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, bonds rose after Bernanke testified to the U.S. House after briefing the Senate on Wednesday. He echoed dovish comments regarding inflation, adding that there has been some reduction in U.S. demand for oil, warning that there could be significant consequences on inflation and growth if oil prices rise much more.  \par \par \uc2 Local gainers included the Discount bond in pesos, last quoted at ARS120.40, up from ARS119.50, and the Par in pesos, which rose to ARS47 from ARS46.70. The Bogar 2018, another peso note, was at ARS145.10, up from ARS144.60 in stronger volume.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't know if the higher volumes correspond to the new rule allowing AFJPs (pension funds) to buy the Bogars, but volumes have been improving," said Sebastian Di Nucci, a portfolio manager with local FDI Consultants.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Thursday, Argentine Economy Minister Felisa Miceli called on Mercosur trade bloc nations to join Argentina and Venezuela in a plan to issue a "Bond of the South."  \par \par \uc2 Miceli spoke in the Argentine provincial city of Cordoba, where economy ministers from the region were meeting ahead of a Mercosur presidential summit on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "We offer interested countries to join us in this action, which will be concluded by the end of September," Miceli was quoted as saying.  \par \par \uc2 Argentine and Venezuelan officials first announced the "Bond of the South" plan earlier this month. The plan has been met with skepticism by some fund managers, who are waiting for more details about how the bonds will work.  \par \par \uc2 Di Nucci compared the hypothetical bond to the Boden 2015, which wasn't trading on Thursday. The Argentine government issued the Boden 2015 earlier this year in a direct sale to the Venezuelan government. Such bond issues represent a more political than financial strategy, Di Nucci said.  \par \par \uc2 Such issues, "augment the Argentine government's debt, but aren't attractive to individual investors for the lack of liquidity they have because they are so concentrated in the hands of one creditor," Di Nucci noted.  \par \par \uc2 In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the peso edged slightly lower on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying. The peso gained early, leading the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start buying dollars at ARS3.0785, said Hector Blanco, a trader with local brokerage ABC Mercado de Cambio.  \par \par \uc2 Peso demand also ebbed somewhat as cereal exporters eased repatriations of overseas dollar earnings, Blanco added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 20-07-06 1925GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1833  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060720e27k000zh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
