\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Argentina's Redrado eyes reserves, not rates  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Martin Roberts  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Juni 2006\par 21:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, June 8 (Reuters) - Compared to other central bankers, Argentina's Martin Redrado spends very little time talking about interest rate policy.  \par \par \uc2 Redrado told a conference on Monday that "the power of classic monetary policy instruments (like interest rates), is reduced" in Latin America's third largest economy, which has a "financial market without sufficient depth."  \par \par \uc2 Inflation is running at about 12 percent per year after more than three years of fast economic growth, and rising prices are now a concern, especially as hyperinflation is a recent memory.  \par \par \uc2 But many economists agree that raising interest rates would have little effect on tackling inflation in a country where credit stands at a relatively low 10 percent of gross domestic product, as the banking system is still recovering from economic collapse in 2001-2002.  \par \par \uc2 "It's true Argentina has no reference rate for (monetary) regulation, but rather the amount of money issued," said Aldo Abram, head of the Exante consultancy.  \par \par \uc2 According to the latest available figures from the first quarter, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was to keep the money supply, using the commonly watched M2 yardstick, within a target range of 116.3-126.2 billion pesos ($37.5-40.7 billion).  \par \par \uc2 CONCERNED OVER COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 Harvard-educated Redrado was deputy foreign minister when he replaced Alfonso Prat-Gay, a former J.P. Morgan executive who was seen as market friendly. A former chair of the National Securities Commission, the markets took Redrado's appointment in their stride.  \par \par \uc2 In a recent newspaper column, Redrado said he was more concerned over Latin America's vulnerability to shifts in demand for commodities from China, India and Russia than international interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 In that situation he preferred to prepare for possible external shocks with a "counter-cyclical" policy of building up reserves and a budget surplus.  \par \par \uc2 Since Argentina payed off the final $9.6 billion it owed the International Monetary Fund in January, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boosted its reserves by some $5.6 billion by buying up dollars flooding the country due to strong demand abroad for its commodities.  \par \par \uc2 "With its policy of boosting reserves, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is building real anti-crisis insurance against our recurring external vulnerability," wrote Redrado, whose six-year term began in September 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Some economists worry that the bank's policy of paying for dollars by issuing bonds may check inflation in the short term, but makes it vulnerable to investors taking fright and opting to cash in rather than renew paper.  \par \par \uc2 "It isn't credible to think the bank can maintain that level of debt forever," said independent economist Fabian Prieto. "They must give the money back some time, and I don't know what will happen when the monetary base expands again."  \par \par \uc2 Neil Dougall, head of emerging markets research at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein in London, was concerned the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been effectively softening the peso to help exporters and this could itself stoke inflation.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say that frequent mopping up of dollars by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases the supply of pesos to the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and tends to either make the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaker or prevent it from rising against the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 "At a time when underlying inflation is on the rise, one tool that could be used to dampen inflationary expectations would be to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "If you get a path of rising inflation, that's something that's also potentially destabilizing to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina's peso <.ARSB=> has traded at close to 3.00 to the dollar since late 2003 amid repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-\b \highlight22\uc2 ARGENTINA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -REDRADO (ANALYSIS)|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|E|RBN  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060611e268000hl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
