\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Argentina Bonds Rebound, Peso Flat On Ctrl Bank Actions  \b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2006\par 18:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 OSTDJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2006, Comtex News Network.  All Rights Reserved.  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, May 16, 2006 (DJCS via Comtex) --  \par \par \uc2 Locally-traded Argentine bonds rebounded strongly Tuesday, following the lead of other emerging market assets, as some hopeful news on U.S. inflation helped end a recent selloff.  \par \par \uc2 The Argentine peso, meanwhile, remained in a state of isolation from the rest of the world. Just as it failed to fall in sympathy with the Brazilian real and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they were suffering capital flight in recent days, on Tuesday the peso failed to join their recovery. It closed the day unchanged at ARS3.0425 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The main reason for the peso's stability in a sea of volatility, traders said, is the persistent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy of the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have left the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  so undervalued relative to the real and other competing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that speculators resisted selling it during the recent phase of emerging market jitters.  \par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - thought to be in the order of $100 million, as with prior days - had the opposite effect. It kept the peso from rallying, even as the regular movement of exporter funds continued to flow into the country. Official data on \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected later, after the market close.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  is an isolated country, just as it has been for some time," said Hector Blanco, a trader at ABC Mercado in Buenos Aires.  \par \par \uc2 But that isolation wasn't so apparent in the fixed-income market.  \par \par \uc2 After heavy selling last week and on Monday, Argentine bonds rebounded strongly Tuesday. Investors followed the lead from other countries, where the mood was bolstered by the U.S. April producer prices report, which wasn't so harsh as to ramp up the risk of a bigger hike in U.S. interest rates. And the fact that Argentine bonds had sold off even more strongly than other emerging markets only enhanced their appeal Tuesday, said Noelia Lucini, an analyst at Argentine Capital Markets in Buenos Aires.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was searching for a floor," said Lucini. "So, today we have seen a very strong rebound that is practically across the length of the (yield) curve."  \par \par \uc2 The benchmark discount bond in pesos, which fell about 1% on Monday, was quoted late Tuesday at ARS127.70, up 1.31%. And the discount bond in dollars issued under New York jurisdiction was up 2.01% at a dollar-equivalent ARS96.3.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar-denominated Boden 2012, meanwhile, rose 0.54% to a dollar-equivalent ARS286.40. It was helped by news that Venezuela will buy another $200 million of this issue.  \par \par \uc2 And the peso-denominated Boden 2008 was last quoted at ARS129.40, up 0.33%.  \par \par \uc2 -By Michael Casey, Dow Jones Newswires; 54-11-4313 1918; michael.j.casey@dowjones.com;  \par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires  \par \par \uc2 05-16-06 1630ET  \par \par \uc2 Dokument OSTDJ00020060516e25g004ln\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
