\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Mixed; Peso Steady On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 April 2006\par 23:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates 2nd, final two grafs with year's largest \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Argentine bonds were mixed Monday as peso-denominated notes recovered from last week's profit-taking, while dollar bonds continued to struggle.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Argentine peso ended the day at ARS3.0775, virtually unchanged from Wednesday's close at ARS3.0800, amid solid volume after the long Easter holiday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported later that it engaged in the year's heaviest dollar buying to hold the peso.  \par \par \uc2 The local bond market, however, saw thin trading coming off the four-day weekend, said Noelia Lucini, a fixed-income analyst with Capital Markets Argentina. Peso-denominated bonds rose after a strong correction last week that was sparked by investor concerns about rising U.S. Treasurys yields, she said.  \par \par \uc2 The restructured par bond in pesos was recently quoted at ARS47.90, up from last Wednesday's close of ARS47.60, while the discount in pesos was at ARS124.50, up from ARS123.30.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar-denominated bonds didn't fare as well. Most notably, the Boden 2012 slipped to ARS283.75 from ARS284.40.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are taking precaution in the local market," Lucini said. "But there will be a recovery this week."  \par \par \uc2 She added that investors will be on the lookout for U.S. inflation data and Tuesday's release of the U.S. Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee meeting minutes from March for cues on where U.S. interest rates are headed.  \par \par \uc2 In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, dollars continued to flow into the country as cereal exporters repatriated overseas earnings amid the year's main soybean harvest.  \par \par \uc2 "There wasn't a lot of movement because people were returning from vacation, but exporters were busy," said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Fernando Izzo of local ABC Mercado de Cambio.  \par \par \uc2 Though the market got off to a slow start, volume built to reach a solid $248 million. To maintain a weaker peso - a goal of government planners who seek to favor Argentine exports - the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up its regular \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported later Monday that it bought $95.5 million in the open market, up from $82.5 million during the previous session. That marked the biggest single-day buy since Dec. 15, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchased $100 million.  \par \par \uc2 -By Kim-Mai Cutler, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4315-9697; argentina@dowjones.com [ 17-04-06 2137GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1833 5054  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060417e24h000hb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
