\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Mixed;Peso Flat In Tight Liquidity  \b0\par\par\uc2 309 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 22:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Locally traded Argentine bonds were mixed Friday, as long-term bonds slid on profit-taking while short-term bonds continued to see gains.  \par \par \uc2 The Argentine peso held steady at ARS3.0750, after ending a mild rally earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 In the bond market, volume slid from peak levels Thursday to an estimated ARS400 million as longer-term peso notes followed U.S. Treasurys prices downward, according to Javier Salvucci, an analyst with Silver Cloud Advisors.  \par \par \uc2 The restructured par bond in pesos was recently quoted at ARS53.00, down from ARS53.20, while the discount bond was down to ARS128.90 from ARS129.00. Gainers along the shorter end of the curve included the Boden 2008, which was up to ARS128.30 from ARS127.90.  \par \par \uc2 Emerging market bonds had reacted positively Thursday to reports of more moderate inflation in the U.S. But confidence waned on Friday, Salvucci said. "We're entering a time of optimism, but the American data still isn't totally convincing."  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso remained unchanged amid continued tight liquidity. The overnight call rate was inching slightly higher to 11%, up from 10.5% on Wednesday, Salvucci noted.  \par \par \uc2 Operating volumes were down on the day in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, coming in at $320 million, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Gustavo Quintana of local Lopez Leon Brokers.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported later Friday that it bought $45.5 million and EUR1 million in the open market, after buying $27.5 million and EUR51 million on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We'll probably see more volume next week with an influx of money from exporters and stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Quintana said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Kim-Mai Cutler, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; argentina@dowjones.com [ 17-03-06 2137GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1833  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060317e23h000wa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
