\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Bonds Begin Turnaround; Peso Stronger  \b0\par\par\uc2 522 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2006\par 23:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--Locally traded Argentine bonds, taking a lead from U.S. Treasurys, began to recover amid low volumes Tuesday after a recent wave of profit taking.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the peso continued to slowly firm against the dollar, strengthening to ARS3.0775 from Monday's close at ARS3.0800.  \par \par \uc2 "It looks like the market has hit bottom and some bonds are beginning to pick up," said Paulino Seoane, a fixed-income analyst in Buenos Aires with Lopez Leon Brokers. Among restructured gainers, the discount in pesos was last quoted at ARS127.00, up from ARS126.70 on Monday, while the par in pesos was unchanged at ARS52.00. Some dollar notes were also improving, with the Boden 2013, a pre-debt swap bond, up to ARS264.01 from ARS263.80.  \par \par \uc2 Trading was still somewhat thin, however, as local investors looked to get a better read on where the U.S. market is headed, Seoane said. U.S. Treasury prices rallied across the curve Tuesday as investors moved from bearish positions after the government released mixed reading on retail sales.  \par \par \uc2 In the local \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, trading was also thin, with weak \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to trader Hector Blanco of local ABC Mercado de Cambio. "The bank bought sporadically, but it wasn't a very aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Blanco said. He said that total volume was around $110 million. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported later Tuesday that it didn't buy any dollars, but instead bought a solid EUR50.5 million.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  regularly buys dollars to maintain a weaker peso and rebuild reserves tapped in January to pay down Argentina's entire $9.5 billion debt with the International Monetary Fund. The constant pumping of pesos into the market has led to some concerns about inflation. In a move that could ease those concerns somewhat, the Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced late Monday that it increased minimum reserve requirements for bank's savings and checking deposits and ended the practice of paying interest on those funds. The change is expected to tighten liquidity conditions and could promote longer-term saving and lending.  \par \par \uc2 According to a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  statement, banks are required as of April 1 to hold 17% of their checking and savings deposit funds in reserve. Savings and checking account reserve ratios had been cut to 14% from 15% in December in a bid to boost liquidity for the summer vacation period.  \par \par \uc2 All fixed term deposits, or certificates of deposit, will maintain their reserve ratios at existing levels. These ratios range from 14% for CDs of up to 29 days to 1% for CDs of between 180 days and 365 days in length. For any deposits held for more than a year, banks are free of any reserve requirement.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said that the increase in reserve ratios implies an additional monetary absorption from the financial system on the order of ARS1.35 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson and Kim-Mai Cutler, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 14-03-06 2222GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1833  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020060314e23e0012i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
