\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Argentina trade surplus thins despite record exports  \b0\par\par\uc2 519 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Januar 2006\par 20:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2006 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Adds details, quote and 2006 forecasts)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Jan 31 (Reuters) - Argentine exports climbed to a record high in 2005 on rising prices for natural resources, but the trade surplus shrank due to strong import growth in the country's third year of sizzling economic expansion, according to government data released on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 President Nestor Kirchner is seen winning political points with the export boom, billed as a result of his government's policy of maintaining a weak peso \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <ARSB=> to favor exports and win new markets.  \par \par \uc2 The trade surplus narrowed last year to $11.32 billion from $12.11 billion in 2004 on exports of $40.01 billion and imports of $28.69 billion, the Economy Ministry said in a report.  \par \par \uc2 In December, the trade surplus rose to $1.03 billion against $854 million in the same month a year earlier, beating analysts' median forecast of $879 million in a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  poll on Jan. 4.  \par \par \uc2 Rising prices for Argentina's agricultural products, fuels and copper boosted exports in the month by 19 percent over December 2004 to $3.53 billion, also above analysts' median estimate of $3.44 billion.  \par \par \uc2 In all of 2005, exports swelled both in terms of revenue and volume as Argentina's economy, Latin America's third-largest, grew by an estimated 9 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the big story in last year's 16 percent export growth was the growing presence of the manufacturing sector.  \par \par \uc2 " Cars have grown a lot due to an accord with Mexico and some sales to Brazil as well. The steel sector has also grown due to prices, not just volume," said Maria Castiglioni, an economist with Asesores Economicos consulting firm.  \par \par \uc2 "With this exchange rate, there are industrial sectors that are going to continue to do well and export," he added.  \par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2  has prevented the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening, keeping it at roughly 3 per U.S. dollar. The peso closed at 3.07/3.0725 per dollar on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The Economy Ministry said exports that grew the most in terms of absolute value in 2005 were vehicles and vehicle parts, followed by seed and soybean sales, mainly to China.  \par \par \uc2 Imports in Argentina have been gradually recovering from a near-freeze in 2002 when prices on foreign goods shot up because of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation.  \par \par \uc2 Imports in December jumped 18 percent to $2.49 billion and in the year imports climbed 28 percent to $28.69 billion, both slightly below forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 The ministry said imports grew in all economic sectors last year, but values rose most for goods related to the automotive industry, cellular phones, data processing equipment and fuels such as natural gas.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  estimates a 2006 trade surplus of $11.5 billion, according to a report in December.  \par \par \uc2 The government forecasts economic growth this year of 4.0 percent while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  puts its expansion at 6.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ARGENTINA-TRADE (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|E|RBN|D|M|RNP  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020060131e21v001w5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex