\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: \highlight22\uc2 Argentina \highlight\uc2  Peso Weaker On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2 ; Bonds Mixed  \b0\par\par\uc2 543 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2005\par 23:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates third graf with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying $100 million during session)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--The Argentine \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market Thursday to weaken the peso to ARS3.0150 against the dollar, delivering a clear message that the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set the previously off-limits 3-to-1 mark as the new ceiling for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the local bond market saw mixed results amid sluggish volume.  \par \par \uc2 The peso weakened nearly two full centavos from Wednesday's close at ARS2.9975. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reported that it bought $100 million, just up from Wednesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of $99 million.  \par \par \uc2 "Clearly (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) doesn't want the peso stronger than ARS3," said Daniel Bou Kahir, a trader with local Banco de la Pampa. "The band between ARS2.90 and ARS3.00 no longer appears to be the (Economy) Ministry target." Operating volume was strong, with cereal exporters stepping in to repatriate overseas dollar earnings in heavier numbers once the peso crossed ARS3.00, he added.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buys dollars to maintain a weaker peso that favors Argentine exports. But stepping up the dollar buying just as inflationary concerns begin to take off should do little to ease analysts' concerns about where the economy is headed. Inflation rose 1.2% on-month in November and it is expected to come in above 12% for 2005.  \par \par \uc2 In the local bond market, longer-term bonds were down, while short-term notes saw some gains, said analyst Javier Salvucci with local Silver Cloud Advisors.  \par \par \uc2 Among losers, the restructured discount bond in pesos was down to ARS118.50 from ARS119.10 on Wednesday, while the par in pesos was at ARS48.60 from ARS49.00. On the upside, the pre-debt swap Bocon Pro 11 was at ARS177 from ARS176.50, while the Bocon Pre 8 was at ARS193.80 from ARS193.30.  \par \par \uc2 The losses on the longer end of the curve likely followed on lower U.S. Treasurys prices, while inflation-adjusted bonds on the shorter end continued to do well on continued increases in the consumer price index, Salvucci said. Just how far inflation can rise before the CPI-linked bonds lose their luster is "the million dollar question," Salvucci said.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there was only muted reaction to late-session news from the national statistics agency, Indec, that gross domestic product rose 2.3% in the third quarter from the second quarter and was up 9.2% on the year, Salvucci said.  \par \par \uc2 On an accumulated basis, growth was also 9.2% for the nine months to the end of September compared with the same nine-month period in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 Economists had expected to see an 8.5% year-on-year growth rate for the quarter. And an earlier one-month economic activity index for September that acts as an initial proxy indicator of GDP had estimated the growth rate for the accumulated nine-month period at 9%.  \par \par \uc2 The result puts Argentina on target to achieve economic growth of around 9% for the third consecutive year in 2005 since its financial crisis of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 -By Drew Benson, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3127; andrew.benson@dowjones.com [ 15-12-05 2238GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 1833  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020051215e1cf00139\par }\