\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:Argentina Peso Sees Light Gain; Ctrl Bk Caps Rally  \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Dezember 2005\par 22:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Updates with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peso strengthened a touch against the dollar Wednesday, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wading into the session to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from extending its one-day rally too far.  \par \par \uc2 The peso ended local trading at ARS2.9925 to the dollar, just stronger than its ARS2.9975 level a day earlier. Tuesday's session had ended a three-day decline against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that saw the peso slip past the psychologically important level of ARS3.00.  \par \par \uc2 Trader Daniel Bou-Kahir of Banco de la Pampa said the peso had been gradually gaining against the dollar for most of the session before the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  entered the market to buy pesos, seeking to keep the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around recent levels. Ten minutes before trading closed, the peso had been at ARS2.99.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $44 million Monday and $43.5 million Tuesday. Official data showed the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought $41 million Wednesday, on par with levels seen earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, liquidity remains healthy as a result of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure to lower minimum cash ratios.  \par \par \uc2 "We see that many companies still have liquidity and are not requiring credit for their (end-year) payments," Bou-Kahir said.  \par \par \uc2 The end of the year traditionally bolsters the peso, as companies needing liquidity to pay bonuses and settle accounts creates higher, seasonal demand for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the local fixed-income market, worries over consumer prices continue to drive investors into peso bonds that are adjusted for inflation. The discount bond in pesos was quoted at ARS120, compared with ARS119.9 a day earlier. The par bond in pesos, another inflation-linked note, was ARS48.30, just higher than ARS48.20.  \par \par \uc2 Analyst Alejandro Barros of Banco Piano said fixed-income investors have largely shrugged at the government's efforts to broker price accords with specific sectors such as supermarkets, seeing these agreements as ineffective.  \par \par \uc2 The primary concern remains the government's commitment to its monetary policy and whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue buying dollars to maintain the exchange rate around ARS3.00, which the government favors to keep exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 "If there's an issue that keeps getting thrown out there with regards to inflation, it's the exchange rate," Barros said. "The dollar should theoretically be at ARS2.40, ARS2.50. To maintain the dollar at ARS3.00, (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) has to bring a lot of pesos to the market."  \par \par \uc2 Last week, the peso's decline sent dollar bond prices higher and put light pressure on peso bonds. Barros said dollar-denominated bonds were seeing some minor declines Wednesday, though prices should hold steady in the near term.  \par \par \uc2 The Boden 2012 was recently quoted at ARS270.5, down slightly from ARS271.00. The Boden 2013 was at ARS243.40, down from ARS244.25.  \par \par \uc2 -By Wailin Wong, Dow Jones Newswires; 5411-4311-3125; wailin.wong@dowjones.com [ 07-12-05 2132GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Do