\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-Argentina post-election economy: more of the same?  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Louise Egan  \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2005\par 19:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)  \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Argentina, Oct 24 (Reuters) - Argentine President Nestor Kirchner is likely to keep playing hard ball with the IMF and big business after a resounding victory in Sunday's mid-term elections, but may soon face some tough decisions to sustain the country's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 Kirchner bolstered his support in Congress and wrestled power away from a rival faction of the Peronist Party in a vote that centered on a Senate race between his wife and a former first lady to represent Buenos Aires, the country's heavyweight province.  \par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, Kirchner won crowds with his habitual sharp-tongued attacks on the International Monetary Fund and foreign investors, including giant Western European utility companies, while taking credit for a three-year economic rebound he said was possible because of snubbing the standard free market policies pushed by Wall Street.  \par \par \uc2 While Argentines wondered whether their leader would adopt a more moderate stance once the election was out of the way, analysts say he will likely continue with an approach on the foreign front that appears to have worked so far.  \par \par \uc2 "He's convinced he did things right and as a result we have this economic recovery ... So why would there be any need to change course?" said Aldo Abram, an analyst with Exante consultancy.  \par \par \uc2 Kirchner needed to triumph in the polls after taking office in 2003 with just 22 percent of the vote when his runoff contender quit.  \par \par \uc2 With 97 percent of the ballots counted, his wife, Cristina, thumped her foes by winning 46 percent, 26 points more than wife of former President Eduardo Duhalde. The president's left-of-center Peronist camp clinched 43 percent of seats in the lower house.  \par \par \uc2 Kirchner was helped by an economic boom now in its third year after a devastating 2001-2002 economic crash that forced a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluation and massive debt default. The economy is growing at around 9 percent for the third year running.  \par \par \uc2 SLEEPLESS IN BUENOS AIRES  \par \par \uc2 But now runaway inflation is menacing that recovery. Kirchner admitted in a radio interview last week he is "losing sleep" over price hikes and Economy Minister Roberto Lavagna is expected to make fighting inflation a top priority.  \par \par \uc2 There is also growing unease about \b \highlight22\uc2 Argentina\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary expansion and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the peso from strengthening, which critics say is inflationary. A stronger peso could reduce the price of imported goods, but at the cost of making Argentine goods less competitive abroad.  \par \par \uc2 Consumer prices are headed this year toward 10 percent growth -- the first bout of double-digit inflation in three years, a troubling prospect for the 38 percent of Argentines living in poverty.  \par \par \uc2 To combat inflation, Kirchner may have to backtrack on some policies and stifle labor demands for higher wages or allow the peso to appreciate, economists believe.  \par \par \uc2 "The prolonged bout of inflation above 10 percent is not stable: it is likely to lead to a dangerous inflationary spiral if the monetary and fiscal authorities do not move to contain it," said Gray Newman, economist at Morgan Stanley in New York.  \par \par \uc2 And it is still not clear whether a poltically fortified Kirchner will agree to immediate loan talks with the IMF or speed up stalled negotiations with disgruntled utility companies to end a 2002 rate freeze.  \par \par \uc2 Major utility companies, such as Suez of France, have opted to pull out of Argentina over the rate conflict and others like Spain's Telefonica and Telecom Italia are still negotiating.  \par \par \uc2 "There won't be an accord with the IMF, there won't be any utility rate increases and on the inflation issue, he will continue to put pressure the price-makers but not act on the monetary and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy," said Buenos Aires political analyst Rosendo Fraga.  \par \par \uc2 But Credit Suisse First Boston analyst Carola Sandy said in a report last week a Kirchner victory is a "positive or neutral market event" that signals a possible fiscal and monetary tightening and renewed interest in negotiations with the IMF, which it owes $11 billion.  \par \par \uc2 Argentina suspended talks with the IMF last year to concentrate on restructuring $100 billion in defaulted debt. The lender has repeatedly said in the past few weeks it is ready to meet with the Argentines and Buenos Aires is expected to respond in the coming days. [20051024 170132 GMT]  \par \par \uc2 [20051024 165245 GMT]   \par \par \uc2 ECONOMY-ARGENTINA-ELECTIONS (PICTURE) (REPEAT)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|M|D|O|OIL|G|RNP|DNP|PGE|PEN|ECONOMY-ARGENTINA-ELECTIONS (PICTURE)|LANGEN|ABN|E|U|RBN|M|D|O|OIL|RNP|DNP|PEN|chained  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051024e1ao001oi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
